<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=laser">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Laser</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1960">1960</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as optical maser</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A laser is a device that emits light through a process of optical amplification based on the stimulated emission of electromagnetic radiation. The word is an acronym for light amplification by stimulated emission of radiation. The theoretical foundations were laid by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-einstein">Albert Einstein</a> in 1917, and Charles H. Townes and Arthur Leonard Schawlow proposed an optical maser in 1958. On 16 May 1960 Theodore H. Maiman operated the first functioning laser at Hughes Research Laboratories, Malibu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, using a flashlamp-pumped synthetic ruby crystal that produced red light at 694 nanometres. Its first widely noticeable use was the supermarket <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcode">barcode</a> scanner, introduced in 1974.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/laser.jpg" alt="Laser" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 彭嘉傑 · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lasers.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>16 May 1960</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Malibu, California, United States</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Theodore Maiman · Charles H. Townes · Arthur Leonard Schawlow · Gordon Gould</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Gordon Gould filed a patent application in April 1959, which was denied; a patent went to Bell Labs in 1960, provoking a twenty-eight-year legal fight. The question of how to assign credit for inventing the laser remains unresolved by historians.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/quantum-mechanics">Quantum theory<span>1900</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/optical-fiber">Optical fibre<span>1965</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/compact-disc">Compact disc<span>1982</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=laser">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
